  "preamble": "Devoicing of the voiced palatal stop /ɟ/ to /c/. In Khmer it was part of the general loss of voiced stops during the Middle Khmer period (completed between the late 16th and 18th centuries); the lost voicing survives as a vowel-register contrast, since vowels after formerly voiced stops first became breathy and then took on different qualities. In Slovak and Czech it happens synchronically through word-final devoicing, where /ɟ/ ď is pronounced [c], and in Hungarian through regressive voicing assimilation, where gy /ɟ/ becomes [c] before a voiceless obstruent.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Devoicing, Register Split, Voicing Assimilation",
